        "id": 1259838
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-07-18T16:41:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I got confused: is the RESET_MAGIC_MASK and MAGIC_VALUE defined in common inappropriate or the code as it is written?\n\nShould the code be specific to the chip since some chips may reset their BBRAM register on Power-On thus eliminating the need to store magic values? Or are you saying regardless of how a specific chip operates, we should always apply this magic algorithm for BBRAM?",
      "parentUuid": "62301f60_f8bf84c5",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 53,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 53,
        "endChar": 31
      },
      "revId": "633a0a7b10d777f81d2c9615fdcfc21432f275db",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4fd8c960_327c2451",
        "filename": "chip/it83xx/system.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 53,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002124
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-07-18T16:50:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "this is *one* use of bram. There are many other fields of bram used throughout the code. My point is that we should dedicate a bram field that covers *all* of bram to indicate if it is valid. When it isn\u0027t we clear all the fields. The other users (like this one) can just be ignorant of that happening behind the scenes.\n\nside note: If there was a proper register that indicated power on reset of vbat then we\u0027d just use that to clear bram. Instead, we have to use a magic value and check it in bram early during startup.",
      "parentUuid": "58f59a89_91148c86",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 53,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 53,
        "endChar": 31
      },
      "revId": "633a0a7b10d777f81d2c9615fdcfc21432f275db",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
